Coastal wetlands, including salt marshes, mangroves, and swamps, act as natural buffers against the destructive forces of storms. Their ability to mitigate flood damage is not merely a matter of standing between the ocean and the land, but rather a complex interaction of hydrology, physics, and ecology. Understanding this process helps clarify why these ecosystems are critical infrastructure in an era of increasing climate volatility.

